// Ok so,
// ~(a, b) is of type (A,B)/(A',B')
// sets(x: M/(int,int)): M
//
// if .... x is a ptr ...
// so, x could be a simple ptr,
// in which case the contents would be written
// as a tuple
// BUT it could be a compound tuple ptr
// in which case the contents would be written
// piecewise.
//
// is that a problem?
// like am I setting myself up for weird fragility?
//
// anyway, ~(a,b) needs to turn into something like (&a, &b)
// and then a call to `get(x)` will see the tuple, unpack it, and follow the pointers
// (in a c-targetting impl, we'd monomorphise everything, so we can do more direct assignment)
//
// In this system, the only 'cleanup' that needs to happen is actual destructors.
// that feels very nice.
//
// let m = ~(x, y)
// becomes
// m = (&x, &y)
//
// ok but what about
// let m = ~fst(~x)
//
// oooh wait
//
// let x = (1, 2)
// puts (1, 2) in a single slot
// but then doing fst(~x) ends up... hmm.
// I guess we'd do {...x, path: [...x.path, 0]}
// like if x is a tuple of (&a, &b) then we grab &a,
// otherwise we just make the ptr a sub-item.
// Ok that seems like it would work.
//
// first of all fst(~x) becomes fst(&x)
// and then fst returns [ptr, destructor?]
// wher destructor is optional
//
// fn fst(~(a, b)) ~a
// becomes
// fn fst(tmp) {
//  point((a0, b0), tmp)
//  (a0, ())
// }
//
// SO does this preclude @manual lenses?
// I think it might not.
//
// let x = ~fst(~y)
// 10
//
// becomes
// {
//  let (t1, c2) = &fst(&y)
//  point(x, t1)
//  10
// }{swap(y, c2(t1))} // this is the same as usual
//
/*
ok so question: would it make sense to just do a single pass,
evalute while decomposing?
answer: using an IR makes it easier to debug things.
and makes it more likely that I can turn it into C.
which would be fun.

ok so also ... what do we think about doing type inference
post decomposition? is that a good idea?


so

let pat = &id

let (a, b) = &apply(&arg) -> we've got something special going on here.
Should we instead have some kind of fat pointer?
like a gets set to a pointer normally, but there's a 'drop()' fn on it or something?
so we got

{
   let x = &fst(&y)
   10
}{drop(&x)} // this is the same as usual

so type Ptr = {stack: number, name: string, path: number[], drop?: () => void}

and

let y = ~{
    lens x <- 10
    log(x)
}

becomes

let y = &lens(10, fn(x) {
    log(x)
})

and
function lens(value, drop): Ptr {
    let stack = newstack()
    _global_stack[stack].lens = value
    return {stack, name: "lens", drop: () => {
        drop(_global_stack[stack].lens)
    }}
}

yeah that sounds right.
ALSO

function drop(ptr) {
    if (ptr.drop) ptr.drop()
}

AND now for manual

@(A, fn(b) { C })

becomes

manual(ptr, A, fn(b) { C })

where

function manual(ptr, front, back) {
    let stack = newstack()
    _global_stack[stack].manual = front
    return {stack, name: 'manual', drop: () => {
        swap(ptr, back(_global_stack[stack].manual))
    }}
}


hmmmm... I find myself unsatisfied with putting drop on the ptr.
because, it's not actually needed to be on there.
it doesn't need to go everywhere the ptr goes.
it's scoped to a given scope, after all.

so from that angle, it makes sense for the fncall to return a tuple
that lives in normal space.

HOWEVER. It also now makes sense for some vbls to be ... different.
like, we should have a two tiered system. Normal vbls and managed vbls.
Like the (t1, c1) returned from a lens fn don't need to go on the stack,
they're generated vbls that don't have the option of lens shenanigans happening to them.
Also, when we have a vbl that doesn't do anything lensy, we can call it normal.
and not do anything special with it.
a bit of local inference for simplification.
BUT also, we could say "everything is handled uniformly", and then we do an
optimization pass on the backend to simplify vbls that don't need lens madness.
that feels simpler tbh.

{
    let x = ~fst(~y)
    10
}

->

{
   let (x, dropx) = &fst(&y)
   10
}{dropx?())}

does that work?
